.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt CSCODEC 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cscodec
.Nd Cirrus Logic CS42L42 and CS42L83 headphone codecs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "cscodec* at iic?"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver supports headphone playback on the Cirrus Logic CS42L42 and on
the CS42L83 found in Apple Silicon Macs, as a digital audio interface
device for a machine driver such as
.Xr applemacaudio 4 .
.Pp
The codec runs as a clock slave with its bit clock at 12.288 MHz used as
its master clock, 48 kHz, as in the power-up sequence of the data sheet.
The machine driver must keep the bit clock running once it has reported
it; the codec can only lose it after switching back to its internal
oscillator.
.Pp
Headphone output starts muted, with the full scale 6 dB down and the
volume at \-30 dB when first opened.
The volume goes from \-62 dB to 0 dB in 1 dB steps.
.Pp
Plug presence is sensed on the jack's tip and polled.
When a plug arrives while the headphones are not playing, the driver
lets the codec find the headset type and set its microphone switches,
then measures the headphone load and sets the amplifier's compensation
for it.
All are reported by
.Xr sysctl 8 :
.Bl -tag -width "hw.cscodec0.headset"
.It Va hw.cscodec0.plugged
1 while a plug is in.
.It Va hw.cscodec0.headset
0 while the type is not known, 1 for a headset with its microphone on
pin 4, 2 for one with it on pin 3 (the OMTP pinout), 3 for headphones
without a microphone and 4 for an optical plug.
.It Va hw.cscodec0.load
The load resistance found, in ohms: 15, 30 or 3000; 0 while not known.
.El
.Pp
Opened for recording with a headset found (types 1 and 2), the codec
records its microphone into the time slot the machine driver gives.
.Pp
The device tree property
.Dq cirrus,ts-inv
gives the kind of jack switch, as in its binding.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr applemacaudio 4 ,
.Xr iic 4 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Nx 12.0 .
.Sh CAVEATS
The CS42L83 has no public data sheet; the driver runs it as a CS42L42,
as other systems do, writing every register it relies on rather than
assuming reset values.
Headset buttons are not reported.
